@page "/alert"

<CatHead Title="Alerts">
    An inline banner that explains a state, in place, on the page. Four variants, same semantic
    families as the buttons and badges.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-error-warning-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">An alert is a condition, not an event</span>
        Use one for something that stays true while the page is open &mdash; degraded
        connection, read-only mode, an empty filter result. Transient feedback about an action the user
        just took belongs in a <a href="/toast">toast</a>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Alert.Variants" Title="Variants" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Always pair the colour with an icon — colour alone is not an accessible signal.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Alert.Action" Title="With an action" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.alert</code> is a flex row, so <code>margin-left:auto</code> on a trailing button
        pushes it to the end.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Alert.ReadOnlyMode" Title="Read-only mode" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>The pattern for telling a viewer-role user why every control is disabled.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Alert.ValidationSummary" Title="A validation summary" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One alert at the top of the form saying nothing was saved, with a link to each field that
        needs attention. It does not replace the per-field messages: each control keeps
        <code>aria-invalid</code> and an <code>aria-describedby</code> pointing at its own
        <a href="/form-validation"><code>.form-error</code></a>, because that is what is read when focus
        arrives.
    </p>
</CatExample>
